// Deutsch-Jozsa with a constant oracle.
//
// Concept
//   The same circuit as `deutsch-jozsa.qf`, but the oracle implements the
//   constant function f(x) = 1. Reading 00 proves the function is constant;
//   reading anything else proves it is balanced. One query decides it.
//
// Expected quantum behaviour
//   A constant f contributes the *same* phase to every input branch, which is a
//   global phase and therefore unobservable. The input register is returned
//   exactly to |00> by the final Hadamards.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   `result` is 00 in 100% of shots.
//
// Mathematical interpretation
//   f(x) = 1 for all x, so (-1)^f(x) = -1 uniformly. The input register state
//   is -1 times the equal superposition, and H on each qubit maps the equal
//   superposition back to |00>. The leading minus sign is a global phase: it
//   changes no probability.
//
// @expect qubits=3 clbits=2 gates=7
// @expect-outcome 00

qubit[3] q;
bit[2] result;

x q[2];
h q[2];

h q[0];
h q[1];

// Oracle for the constant function f(x) = 1: flip the output regardless of x.
x q[2];

h q[0];
h q[1];

measure q[0] -> result[0];
measure q[1] -> result[1];
